<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dachau concentration camp</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1933">1933</a>–<a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dachau <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/concentration-camp">concentration camp</a>, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>, was the first regular Nazi concentration camp, opened in March 1933 for political prisoners. It became the model for the SS camp system and later held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romani-people">Roma</a>, clergy, Soviet prisoners of war and others from across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>; more than 41,000 people died there. American troops liberated it on 29 April 1945.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dachau-concentration-camp.jpg" alt="Dachau concentration camp" width="320" height="231" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:KZDachau1945.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The camp stood on the grounds of an abandoned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a> and munitions factory northeast of the medieval town of Dachau, about 16 kilometres northwest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>. After the takeover of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> on 9 March 1933, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-himmler">Heinrich Himmler</a>, then Chief of Police in Munich, toured the site to see whether it could hold prisoners in protective custody. It opened on 22 March 1933 with about 200 prisoners, and Himmler announced it as the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/concentration-camp">concentration camp</a> for political prisoners, able to hold 5,000 people. It remained in operation until April 1945, nearly all of the twelve years of the Nazi regime.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>At first the camp held communists, Social Democrats and other opponents of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-party">Nazi Party</a>. Its purpose widened to forced labour and then to the imprisonment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romani-people">Romani</a>, Germans and Austrians whom the party regarded as criminals, and finally of foreign nationals from occupied countries. Clergy imprisoned for opposing the regime, mainly Catholic, were also held there. The camp trained guards of the SS-Totenkopfverbände and became a model for other camps. Its iron gate bore the motto Arbeit macht frei, and forced labour soon became a method of torture and murder.</p><p>Over 4,000 Soviet prisoners of war were shot at the SS range at Hebertshausen from 25 November 1941. Hundreds of prisoners suffered and died in medical experiments, among them hypothermia experiments under Sigmund Rascher in which nearly 100 died. From 1944 the camp system grew to include nearly one hundred sub-camps near armaments factories. In April 1945 Himmler forbade handing the camp to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a>. American forces liberated the main camp on 29 April 1945, and about 10,000 of the roughly 30,000 prisoners were then sick.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>After the war the site held SS men awaiting trial, then from 1948 ethnic Germans expelled from eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and for a time a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> military base. It closed in 1960. Former prisoners joined to erect a memorial on the site, whose display was reworked in 2003 and follows the path of new arrivals. Two barracks have been rebuilt, the other thirty are marked by low cement curbs filled with pebbles, and visitors may see the crematorium ovens. The memorial site is open to the public.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dachau concentration camp” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Terror was organised by the SS under Heinrich Himmler, which absorbed the police, ran the Gestapo and the concentration camps from <b>Dachau</b>, opened in March 1933, onwards, and in 1939 combined its security services in the Reich Security Main Office under Reinhard Heydrich.</blockquote><a href="/empire/nazi-germany">Nazi Germany · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
